  "note": "紅茶の雑学: the Japanese name 紅茶 comes from the colour of the brewed liquor (水色), not the leaves; English 'black tea' is named after the dark leaves; in China 黒茶 is a different, post-fermented tea (プーアル茶). Red pigment = theaflavins from oxidation (発酵) of catechins. Readings: 紅→くれない, 茶葉→ちゃば, 淹れた→いれた, 水色→すいしょく, 黒茶→くろちゃ (こくちゃ was heard as 緑茶 by Scribe in both take-1/2 clips), 紅色→べにいろ.",
  "sources": [
    "Japan Tea Association (via furunavi.jp/discovery/?p=7785, macaro-ni.jp/27274): the Japanese name 紅茶 derives from the 水色 (liquor colour), not the leaf colour; English 'black tea' from the dark leaves",
    "macaro-ni.jp/143273, serai.jp/?p=145970: 紅茶 = fully fermented tea, liquor red-brown; brewed in soft water the liquor turns a clear orange-red",
    "jsbba.or.jp/wp-content/uploads/file/39-40(1).pdf, nagasaki-u.ac.jp 175 paper: theaflavins are the bright red pigments of black tea, formed when catechins are enzymatically oxidised after rolling",
    "kotobank.jp/word/中国茶, allabout.co.jp/gm/gc/403129: Chinese six tea classes 緑・白・黄・青・紅・黒; 黒茶 (dark tea) is microbially post-fermented, e.g. プーアル茶; 紅茶 = black tea",
    "Naming by 多田元吉 is only a tradition (no primary source) — left out of the script"
  ],
